# OmniVision OV5647 MIPI CSI-2 camera sensor driver.
#
############################################################################

config VIDEO_OV5647
	bool "OV5647 camera sensor"
	default n
	depends on DRIVERS_VIDEO
	select I2C
	---help---
		Enable the OmniVision OV5647 MIPI CSI-2 camera sensor driver.

		The driver implements the NuttX capture framework's sensor side
		(struct imgsensor_s), so the board registers it together with a
		SoC-side imgdata driver:

		  ov5647_initialize(i2c, OV5647_I2C_ADDR);
		  capture_register("/dev/video0", imgdata, sensors, 1);

		The sensor outputs RAW10 Bayer data over two MIPI CSI-2 data
		lanes.  Its control interface is SCCB, which is an I2C subset the
		standard NuttX I2C driver drives directly.

		Seven modes are implemented, in four geometries, and the
		application chooses one for each stream it opens.  The board
		reconfigures its VICAP/CSI path to match; see the board's
		KICKPI_K7 camera source.

		  1920x1080 @ 15   the middle of the array, read at full
		                   resolution and cropped
		  1296x960 @ 30    the 2x2 bin of the whole array
		  1296x960 @ 15
		  1280x720 @ 30    that binned readout, cropped to 16:9
		  1280x720 @ 15
		  640x480  @ 60    the binned array, subsampled
		  640x480  @ 30

		Each geometry runs at two rates, and the slower entry of a pair is
		the one that can expose a darker scene: the frame is longer, and an
		exposure cannot outlast the frame it is in.  A pair differs in
		nothing else -- the two entries share a register set and a link
		rate -- so moving between them costs no clock, no D-PHY rate and no
		buffers.  Reaching the slower rate of the three faster geometries
		does need a longer line as well as a longer frame, because their
		vertical total is eleven bits and caps a frame at 2047 lines.

		The 1920x1080 mode is the one that reads the middle of the pixel
		array at full resolution, with no binning.  It therefore collects
		less light per pixel than the binned modes do -- about 0.9 of a stop
		less than 1296x960, after taking the longest exposure its 15 fps
		readout allows -- and it shows less of the scene, because it crops.
		What it gives in exchange is 1.67 times the pixels in a 16:9 shape.
		It is also the only geometry that changes the capture geometry
		without changing the MIPI link rate.

		The 1280x720 mode changes the geometry without changing the link
		rate either, but it gets there the other way: it keeps the binned
		readout and crops it to 16:9.  That costs a quarter of the vertical
		field of view and no light at all, and it cuts the pixels every
		stage behind the sensor has to move to 74 per cent -- which is what
		it is for, since the 1296x960 modes were measured running slightly
		over their own frame period.  It is also the geometry the encoder
		needs no padding or cropping for.

		None of that is configurable here, and one part of it is worth
		saying why.  The driver has to name a mode for the sensor to sit in
		before an application has asked for anything -- a capture device
		has to exist before it can be asked for a format, and the engine
		behind this sensor has no scaler, so a device node implies a
		geometry.  That mode is OV5647_MODE_DEFAULT in ov5647.h, and it is
		not a Kconfig setting because it is not a decision: the table's
		first entry, for the reason its comment gives.  Which mode a stream
		runs at is the application's to choose, and it says so before it
		opens the camera.
if VIDEO_OV5647

config OV5647_I2C_ADDR
	hex "OV5647 I2C (SCCB) slave address"
	default 0x36
	range 0x08 0x77
	depends on VIDEO_OV5647
	---help---
		Seven-bit SCCB address.  The OV5647's factory default is 0x36,
		which is what a Raspberry Pi compatible module uses.

config OV5647_I2C_FREQUENCY
	int "OV5647 I2C (SCCB) bus frequency"
	default 100000
	depends on VIDEO_OV5647
	---help---
		SCCB is specified for 100 kHz.  The sensor tolerates more on most
		modules, but the specification has no acknowledgement bit, so a
		faster clock leaves less margin for the receiver to sample it
		reliably.

endif # VIDEO_OV5647
